==Scottish Cup==
{{Main|2018–19 Scottish Cup}}
On 24 November, Celtic were drawn to face [[Airdrieonians F.C.|Airdrieonians]] at [[Celtic Park]] in the fourth round of the [[2018–19 Scottish Cup]].<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/15329 | title=Celtic face Airdrieonians at home in Scottish Cup fourth round | publisher=[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] | date=24 November 2018 | access-date=24 November 2018 | archive-date=25 November 2018 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181125030833/http://www.celticfc.net/news/15329 | url-status=live }}</ref> A [[Scott Sinclair]] double and a debut goal for [[Timothy Weah]] secured a 3–0 win.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/15632 | title=Manager: Plenty of positives for Celts in Scottish Cup win | publisher=[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] | date=19 January 2019 | access-date=20 January 2019 | archive-date=21 January 2019 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190121064236/http://www.celticfc.net/news/15632 | url-status=live }}</ref> On 20 January 2019, Celtic were drawn to face [[St Johnstone F.C.|St Johnstone]] in the fifth round.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/15634 | title=Celtic face St Johnstone at home in Scottish Cup fifth round | publisher=[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] | date=20 January 2019 | access-date=20 January 2019 | archive-date=21 January 2019 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190121064239/http://www.celticfc.net/news/15634 | url-status=live }}</ref> [[Scott Sinclair]]'s hat-trick was coupled by [[Scott Brown (footballer, born June 1985)|Scott Brown]]'s first goal in two years, and a [[James Forrest (footballer, born 1991)|James Forrest]] strike, which sealed Celtic's place in the quarter-finals.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/15747 | title=Manager: Excellent second-half display crucial in fifth-round win | publisher=[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] | date=10 February 2019 | access-date=11 February 2019 | archive-date=11 February 2019 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190211230050/http://www.celticfc.net/news/15747 | url-status=live }}</ref> On 11 February, Celtic were drawn to face [[Hibernian F.C.|Hibernian]] at [[Easter Road]] in the quarter-finals.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/15755 | title=Celtic face Hibernian in Scottish Cup quarter-final tie | publisher=[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] | date=11 February 2019 | access-date=11 February 2019 | archive-date=12 February 2019 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190212130807/http://www.celticfc.net/news/15755 | url-status=live }}</ref> Goals from [[James Forrest (footballer, born 1991)|James Forrest]] and [[Scott Brown (footballer, born June 1985)|Scott Brown]] secured Celtic's place in the semi-finals.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/15839 | title=Neil Lennon hails magnificent performance in Scottish Cup win | publisher=[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] | date=2 March 2019 | access-date=4 March 2019 | archive-date=6 March 2019 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190306111329/http://www.celticfc.net/news/15839 | url-status=live }}</ref> On 4 March, Celtic were drawn to face [[Aberdeen F.C.|Aberdeen]] or [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]] in the semi-finals.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/15845 | title=Scottish Cup semi-final draw | publisher=[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] | date=4 March 2019 | access-date=4 March 2019 | archive-date=6 March 2019 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190306043209/http://www.celticfc.net/news/15845 | url-status=live }}</ref> On 12 March, it was determined that Aberdeen would be Celtic's opponents, having defeated Rangers 2–0 in their quarter-final replay. The Bhoys sealed their place in the final with [[James Forrest (footballer, born 1991)|James Forrest]], [[Odsonne Édouard]] and [[Tom Rogic]] all on the scoresheet.

